#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Builds a block in the guest for every slot of the chain a node follows, and has the node judge it.

    builder/shadow [--slots N] [--gas G] [--ready S] [--process] [--guest-sends] [--log FILE]        (from evm/)

Unless it is told to with --live, nothing here is sent to a relay: this is the builder run in shadow, to
find out whether what the guest builds from pending transactions is valid and whether it is ready in time.

For each new head it gathers what the next slot fixes (from the consensus node: randomness, withdrawals,
the parent beacon root; from a relay's public list: the proposer's fee recipient and registered gas limit),
takes pending transactions from the execution node's pool, has that node trace them to learn what state
they touch and prove that state at the head, and puts the lot to the guest as a request to build
and bid (Evm/Build.lean, Evm/Bid.lean). What comes back is made into a bid submission, signed by the guest
with its own key, and handed to the execution node's own validator,
`flashbots_validateBuilderSubmissionV5`, which is the code relays run; the signature, which that validator
does not look at, is checked with `bid-verify`. When the real next block
arrives, the slot inputs that were guessed are compared with what it had.

This program is the untrusted host. It never goes in an image.

  --slots N    stop after N slots (default: run until interrupted)
  --gas G      the most gas the guest is asked to fill (default: all the block's limit allows)
  --engine E   which of the guest's two builders is asked: fast (the engine of Fast/, a request that
               begins JBLF1; the default, except with --live), jaune (the default with --live), or both. With both, each slot's request is put to
               Jaune's builder and then, the same bytes but for its name, to the fast engine's, with no
               limit of time on either so that each answer depends on the request alone; the two answers
               must be the same bytes, and the record says whether they were and how long each took. For
               comparing, never with --live.
  --ready S    the guest is to have its block back S seconds before the slot (default 2): it is given
               until then, less 1.5 s for ending the block, to go on adding transactions. A process has
               no clock and takes as long as it takes.
  --process    put requests to .lake/build/bin/evm-serve-min instead of a guest in a trust domain
  --log FILE   one line of JSON a slot (default /data/eth/shadow/shadow.jsonl)
  --top-up ETH --wallet KEY.pem [--top-up-priority GWEI]
               put one more transaction in front of what is proposed, from the wallet whose key is in
               KEY.pem: it pays ETH to the block's fee recipient, which is the proposer, so the bid is
               worth that much more, but only in a block with this slot's number and this proposer's fee
               recipient. Anywhere else it reverts, and costs its gas at GWEI a unit over the base fee
               (default 2) and no more. It is signed here for each slot, each time with the wallet's next
               nonce, so of all the copies ever signed at most one can land. It goes nowhere but to the
               guest and the node's validator. Left out, nothing is signed.

  --guest-sends
               the guest posts each block it builds to a relay itself, over TLS (Evm/Send.lean), and this
               program is only told what the relay said. Without --live the relay is a stand-in on this
               machine (builder/standin-relay) and the guest is the shadow image, which believes the
               stand-in (scripts/build-efi tdx evm-os-shadow): nothing leaves this machine. The stand-in
               writes down what it was sent, taken apart, and each record says whether that is the same
               submission, field for field, as this program makes of the guest's answer and has the
               node's validator judge. The guest's two keys are asked for with reports when the run
               begins and kept, with their quotes, beside the log (.keys.json). Not with --attest,
               --process or --engine both.
               With --live and --relays the guest is the image that is meant, and SENDS each block to
               those of the relays named that hold the registration it was built for. The relays must be
               among the four the image has. Nothing is judged by the node first: the guest sends when
               it has built. A block with a sealed transaction in it is never shown to this program,
               which then knows its block in the chain by the payment in it (--top-up).
  --sealed --wallet KEY.pem
               with --guest-sends (which it implies): a transaction nobody but the guest is shown. This
               program, as somebody with a transaction to send, signs a transfer of nothing from the
               wallet to itself, seals it to the guest's sealing key with the page's own script
               (demo/seal.js, under node) and hands the sealed bytes in (Evm/Seal.lean). Then, as the
               host, it goes on asking for blocks as before, never having put the transaction in a
               request; when the guest says what state it lacked for a transaction it left out, that is
               proven too and the block asked for again. A block with the sealed transaction in it is not
               shown to this program: it is read from what the stand-in relay took, and judged by the
               node from that. At the end every byte that crossed the guest's network, and its console,
               are searched for the transaction and for its signature. The transaction is signed and
               goes to the stand-in on this machine; it is never broadcast.
  --proofs --wallet KEY.pem
               with --sealed (which it implies): the sealed transaction brings its own proofs, and this
               program as the host is asked nothing about it. Each new block of the chain is shown to the
               guest with the node's own witness for it, so that the guest follows the chain
               (Evm/Follow.lean). As the sender, this program asks the node for proofs of what its
               transaction reads at the newest block (the wallet's account; and, the transaction being a
               deposit of nothing into the wrapped-ether contract, that contract, its code and the
               wallet's slot in it), and seals them in with it (Evm/Seal.lean, "With proofs"). As the
               host it proves only what the public transactions read. At the end the frames, the
               console and every request and answer are searched as with --sealed, and also for the
               wallet's address and the slot's number, which with proofs brought along the host has no
               occasion to be told.
  --attest     ask the guest for a report with each answer (the challenge is the parent's hash); at the
               end the last block is kept under attested/ with the quote made of its report
  --live --relays URL[,URL...]
               SEND each bid to the relays named, at /relay/v1/builder/blocks: only a block the node's
               validator judged valid, with a signature that verifies, before its slot, and only to
               relays that hold the registration the block was built for. Implies --attest. Stops at the
               first block built here that becomes the chain's head, and keeps under landed/ what shows
               it. Without --live nothing leaves this machine but questions to the relay's public lists.

Settings in the environment: SHADOW_RPC (http://127.0.0.1:8545), SHADOW_BEACON (http://127.0.0.1:5052),
SHADOW_RELAY (https://boost-relay.flashbots.net, whose list of proposers is used when no relays are
named), SHADOW_QGS (/var/run/tdx-qgs/qgs.socket), SHADOW_PROOF_KEYS (1024, the most slots of one account asked of
the node in one call), SILO_TDVF and SILO_HOST_PORT (5035) for the guest.
"""
